<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
Hardened-runtime entitlements for Wormhole (signed with `codesign -o runtime`).

Deliberately ABSENT (not needed, and each weakens the hardened runtime):
- cs.allow-jit / cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory: GPUI renders through Metal with
  shaders precompiled at build time; nothing in the app generates machine code.
- cs.disable-library-validation: the binary links only system frameworks and
  libSystem (check with `otool -L`); no third-party dylibs or plugins are loaded.
- cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables: DYLD_* only affects the app process itself;
  shells spawned in the PTY are separate executables with their own signatures
  and still receive the full environment.
- App Sandbox: a terminal must run arbitrary user programs, so it cannot be sandboxed.

PRESENT: the "resource access" entitlements below. macOS attributes privacy (TCC)
requests from programs running inside a terminal to the terminal app, and under
the hardened runtime such a request is denied outright unless the app holds the
matching entitlement. Each one only makes the normal user-consent prompt possible
(texts in Info.plist); it grants nothing by itself. Same set Ghostty/iTerm2 ship.

CONDITIONAL, deliberately not in this file: scripts/bundle.sh signs with a copy that
scripts/entitlements.sh generates from this one, adding the keys below, and only for a
Developer ID build (APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY) that also has the matching provisioning
profile (APPLE_PROVISIONING_PROFILE_PATH, embedded as Contents/embedded.provisionprofile).
- com.apple.developer.usernotifications.time-sensitive: lets "needs you" notifications use
  the Time Sensitive interruption level (breaks through Focus and the notification
  summary). It is a restricted entitlement: macOS accepts it only when an embedded profile
  authorizes it, and refuses to launch an ad-hoc, unsigned or profile-less build that
  claims it. Hence it cannot live in the base file that every build, including local
  and ad-hoc ones, is signed with. Builds without it deliver those notifications at Active.
- com.apple.application-identifier (<TEAMID>.<bundle id>) and
  com.apple.developer.team-identifier: how macOS ties the signature to the profile; the
  profile's own values, so only valid together with it.
Needs a provisioning profile that grants it; without one the app is signed without it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- `osascript`, `open -a`, editors driving other apps, agent tools using AppleScript. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- CLI tools recording audio (e.g. voice input for coding agents, ffmpeg). -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- CLI tools using the camera (ffmpeg, imagesnap). -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Scripts reading Contacts / Calendars / Photos / Location via their frameworks. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.photos-library</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
